  === Complete Solutions
  #sol-item(1, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Ordering by atomic number removes the anomalies that Mendeleev's mass ordering produced.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Rows are periods; columns are groups.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The outer-shell count decides the chemistry, which is why a group behaves as a family.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The period number is the highest shell occupied.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Group I metals give alkaline solutions with water, hence alkali metals.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The halogen group contains fluorine, chlorine, bromine, iodine and astatine.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Group 0 (or VIII) elements have full outer shells.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A full outer shell means there is no tendency to gain, lose or share electrons.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Reactivity increases down Group I, so caesium is the most reactive of these.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Distance and shielding make the outer electron easier to remove.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Reactivity decreases down Group VII, so fluorine is the most reactive.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The extra shells shield the incoming electron from the nucleus.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A more reactive halogen displaces a less reactive one from its salt.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Bromine gives the solution an orange-brown colour.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Bromine is the only liquid halogen at room temperature; iodine is solid.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Nuclear charge rises while the shell stays the same, so the atoms are pulled in.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Each new period adds a shell, so the atoms get larger.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The outer electron is held more tightly as nuclear charge rises within the same shell.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The outer electron is further out and shielded by more inner shells.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Aluminium's single 3p electron is shielded, and sulphur's paired 3p electron repels its partner.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Elements lose electrons less easily from left to right, so metallic character falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Larger atoms lose their outer electrons more easily, so metallic character rises.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Aluminium oxide reacts with both acids and alkalis.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The oxide of the most electronegative element in Period 3 is the most acidic.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Metal oxides are basic; they dissolve in acid to form salts.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Transition metals show several oxidation states and form coloured compounds.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Iron in the Haber process and vanadium(V) oxide in the contact process are standard examples.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    He predicted eka-silicon, later found to be germanium.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Two outer electrons in the third shell is magnesium.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Argon's electron comes from a full shell and is the hardest of the period to remove.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sodium is 2, 8, 1.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Group II elements have two outer electrons.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Argon has the full configuration 2, 8, 8.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    A new shell is added at each step, so the atoms grow.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Calcium loses two outer electrons to form Ca²⁺.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Sulphur dioxide dissolves to give an acid.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Radius falls across the period, and argon is the last element of it.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "C", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    The halogens become darker, denser and higher melting down the group.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "B", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    Silicon lies on the metal–non-metal boundary.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "D", dense: true, pad: 0.0pt)[
    One outer electron in the fourth shell is potassium.
  ]

]

